        <p>Duch.... of r ork hat inherited the same desire to help the needy. and to the greet popularity of her mother the Princom May owes much of her ow, The Duchom's life wee a continual life of well-doing and no Dorcas could have devoted beret-If more fully its heart and mul to benefiting the poor. Much of her time oho spent in needlework for the poor, till her industry in this direction became • stand- ing Joke in the Royal circle. All who knew the voodooet of her heart mil the widenem of her chanty will mourn her loss, and it may be mot without ma ggeration that in her the poor ham lost one of their best friend..</p>
        <p>The idea of Mere.. ing the military force. of the Crown by some form of compulsory service has I taken. strong bold of military men who devote their wits and time to army reform. There is accordingly to be a great field day 1.000 among them at the Royal United Service Institution, tbat ren- dezvous of all the spirits about the metropolis who are Internet...l in increasing our military strength. Lord Wolseley himself will be in the chair, and the aubject which will be discussed will be the relative ' advantages of voluntary and compulsory service. As • proof that the subject has been long present to the mad of officer. high in position,the Institution chose it as U e theme of the ernsy given out for its annual competition. The competition has now been I adjudicated upon, and it.. to discuen the prize !essay that the Institution will meet in grand form • week hence. As the matter will on dealt with from both • military and national point of view, the debate should be of the greatest importance, an some of thy very beet men, whose military idess are leavening the army, will take part in it Ihe I view, of the military solved will thumbc adequately represented. and it would be interesting if the Chancellor of the Exchequer could be tempted to cross the street and present the question from a civilian standpoint.</p>
        <p>. When what is called • semiofficial Note is rent nut by the French Owernment announcing that the latent news from the Wed Coast of Africa fore- shadows great difficulties, nod when both France and England are hurrying troops to the dieputed territoriee, the position becomen one of critical gravity. The charge in the Note in question, that the Royal Niger Company ha. been inciting the people to rebellion, smunt”B that the territory in French. By all the principles which Mine hitherto regulated internetional relations in reference to hinterland spheres of influence, the French colonial party in the tend of the Niger have been engaged in • process of unmitigated land-grabbing,which is acknowlvdned and ilefended by the French Gov. ernment !twit The remissness of British •uthori- t lee in our territory on the Lower Niger must haw been culpable to an extreme degree; and the men in the Wow will want very specifically to know why the British Mid not a single officer in Boutin or Bikini to represent our rights. It was pomade for French colonial artlonturern and liltbmoter, at the head of • handful of native., to occupy posi- tions which we regarded an British, but which Wowed no trare of British occupation. The fset in that our Colonial Ramiro in too big for one ter, even • Mr. Chamber,ain, to manage, imperially if that klinmt-r has also a domestic social policy to engineer Matter. Imre now reached thin stage that France mum retreat or England must give way, or war is unavoidable. Such • situation could ne•ir have arisen if the Colonial Minister bad not been no engulphed by ilifficultim in the Transvaal that he had not one poor moment to turn Ills eye upon the event, pawing on the Niger.</p>
