        <head>LADIES' LEITEIL</head>
        <p>LADIES' LEITEIL</p>
        <p>We Nun Nowa. A hat, and emart looking one withal, which doem not spoil with the rain, and which fite firmly on to tho head, le an example of heailgear much to be &amp;wired. FAieh hat oan be msdo of straw and chenille in red, with red glace ribbon, •panache of cock'. feathers. It might eipially well be made in purple or blue or green, or any other colour which happeoe to take your fancy.</p>
        <p>During the coming winter velvet h t. win rm. bahly o very fenloonebe. One of the Liest etylee of velvet h ttn ham the brim turned eherply upwarda to chow the lining which might be of black wojiiin-endiroidered, and cinder of ribbon.. Toe clueter of coloured ribbon ie caught with a elect and pante likgger, arid the cached crown ie adorned ,Vitli three plume.. . . . • • •</p>
        <p>A delightfed little travelling gown hos • plaio dinging skirt of line vi,•loli Of L;neoln green. • pouched bodice without buovie, and fast...log at tke aide. This In trimmed iii grmluated black velvet ribbon, and in alit into • nniall V a, the neck to ahoo • vent of men and Wee or to ked washing silk, a neat lawn collar, and a blank natio stock, little white lywn tutnoser sulfa make • pretty linink to the weinta Ting bodice may alternate with • pouched lilotian rather wrong make of green nurah, trimmiel v ; imertion, or merely horizontally tuukal, an I v • with • !dein or jewelled white kid belt.</p>
        <p>A very olegent eluding or w-lking gown Win velvet, with etylidi ali spa " bodice, richly trimmed with chinehilla, and fastened at tall Whit with a belt. Thow who wear the princesl gown will find themeelven fort.' to enliet the aid of —a bustle. Terrible as this may sewn to th of moderate views on (willow, the lott,rire has been gradually coining into ace in Pane during the year, and mow of the really smart Parisian who have worn the pouched coetume no con• tinuouely alma, tho beginning of the year have found it neoewary to intraluce hip padding, to counteract the top heavy clinch of the pouch and the inevitable natrirh feather boa, which is apt to give each a high-shouldered look to the wearer.</p>
        <p>With the popolarity of the Ruittian mole, innotrate blouse and skirt hati rativral sod IL to uinal to wit a VCIVP. Motu, trimmed it.th fur. worn witti • eilk or cloth .km. In autuoir ot ;two Moth are worn with flu, skirt.; of eotre-pon•ling mamar. For young lad... *venni,: gown., ..lotTai 1,1011.01. with Win Ilkirt•O! the Patna tint nre oto, foo,inaahle, th..y are iotr,e. tinuw trimmed with cc in' atut rot.et,toi of adnp.r tint.</p>
        <p>Chiffon bl •usee Cr. war, but the inottnarelp!e foils, which still pre, rail. are applemented by tins borders of for yds, ing and • little very fine gulp..., studded with oii jewels. A favoured model h.&amp; jewelled /run, to soft ehillfun running up [0 the aquae neck, and aught down with • broad lonnl of jswelled passe• menterie. On loth, side xis squals bolero of tine guipure, ei. wit Iwo, work of nor. row lobiek velvet, each .n. 11,1,1,4 fixed sdh • jewel, leaving a looped tots There are throe methods ..fousling fathers lamp them, plset them in curling pine, as used for the hair, an I leave o limn for {ern days then comb earsfully ous '2 la • slightly warm goffering iron or ourling p.n. Do the barbs in Instches before the lips soul shake well this choking is essential). :‘ The usual plan is to place the back to knife, nr evsn a paper knife, under one of the Istrite .of the feeder, close to the shaft, and then plass the thumb over it, and gently draw the knife to the other end of the barb, regulating :he pressure with the thumb. Every barb is done individually in this way. Th. work I. usually carried on on front ot • firs. 00tbad la adopted by profeesional curlers. Your loving J Imola</p>
