r mirubating famine met forth au the pamphlet • referred to ? It a. envie plan -simply the ex- Itension of irrigation to dearicte which am ier ; ;hialule for Im £fll.ctixl with famine. Irrigation has , I already done an iinincese amount of weld 111 India, yot there lias bun • singular slowness 111 plashing , ; forward irrigation works which are necessity to I ere millions of people from the pangs of stares- , Item. Where millions 4/1 pounds have been spent !upon railways only thousands have been eepeisdiel ; ; in providing irrikit.ng and riaeigahile canals. Yet ; there are abundant reasons to prove that canals • will answer the need. of India rich better than railways. 14 I long ago as 10:7, the late fiord • Deer argued that it wr • completo /marl. to *rime tact costly lines cif railway were suitable for India simply lucare they met ; the wants of this oritry. Other great autao- rare have from tone to tune given squat- I sir to siinilsr opinimu. Writing in 14471 ; about the lanolin that then prevailed, Mr.. • N1,,1T1N,161.11 deviated that if the ! nsoney which was spent 111P011 the (annum were' nem' for providing irngation aol navigation the I whole if the .111 clod provinces would be eared • or dearth awl ton to twenty millions I added to the i11141,11(1.4 the people. Hut there line ; elways, it would seem, bees au inexplicable re- luctance or the part of the g,ivorning enthral. ,crc liehe to undertake work. if irrigation. In ; regard to 111.101 of that which has been li•klio their Ihands appear to to•v• been forced by lemma The ' Org. canal. rya I.' 4144nel Cnvie V, we Clue cot- come of the great famine/A the pr 'jut in the Ituli of the farce of I . and the Drus• works of that .41 I°:l A• Joax Hamar robin • speech which he made et Illsochester the Indian government generally traded ontil the burr, was ett4.o beier• they Inched the stable door. It the fart, which is now raging in India PS w the op* •4 the pimple of this wonky to ;</p>
        <p>tho value and tieod of works of irrigation it will not have been ilitirely a misfortune The In. lien romance " which given the 1, tiliphlet we are dealing with int title M really a nano-del the extmordiriary titlectoof the drikitein works which wore carried out in the itmlavery !Alta almut the !middle of the century. In Is id the deplorable condition of the aidavery dlntred was Utmost upon the notice of the Indian Onvertit 11111 l t by the I dwindling away •if the Isimilation and the decline lid the revenue. Tile few irrigateui winks in the district were neglected, and munetinies the no. were withered with dnitight iiml at ether times they were washed away by floods. Captain Correthof the Madras Engineers now General Sir A Arnett t'tryriis I was eventnally tmktel to inveatin gato end mport. Captain l'orron, whit, it may at once be said, is the lion i of the romance, reportml that the district was " a nithle rnfiaoa. of alluvial land, tit fur alimet any enltivat ," and he cog- goats.' a retied startling scheme of irrigation. To use his own words, lie prepneed to ...invert the Rater of the Dodavery --• riot r, liy the way, four nuke in width—into money inetead ii lotting it rim into (Ito sea. Captain I 'orrooO plan, one of the itoldeet of its tool, was adopted and, in spite of otiovinmit didieultion, wan carried a successful issue. The ithangt. toss magical. A population whi...11 had dwlielled hi about halt,. million has now risen to two millions, arid shows a density greater than that cif Iteltnins, the meet populous country in Europe. Famine is now unknown, and the district is described as " th• garden of the grant Northern Province. The revenue has increased beyond all expectation", and Dm district provelm a seen proaperity which can scarcely be equallml 111 any other part or helm iii the whole, therefore, this page in thin r,,,n1 of the Dialavery Delta may he fairly described as one i f the isibliint riimanues of Itritiab ride oi India, and an a viands!