        <head>MESSRS. RICHMOND AND COMPANY,</head>
        <p>MESSRS. RICHMOND AND COMPANY,</p>
        <p>LIMITED. P. VISITOR'X INIVRIffiNIONS OP N ARRINGTON. In last week's" ornmarce " there was an illuotrated article extending to seventeen pager under the title ° Richmond's; in the Fell" in which •Imeat everything of interest concerning Mews. Riehni land Cmenand Limited, of A“drini o.treet, and also ot Stratford end London, is stated. The wilier under the psi thlnnym of Lissar Columbus " says •1 Warrington is not exactly, an ideal town from that point at view of the icithete; but it does its duty well in &amp;misting to keep up our re- putation as • nation of shopkeepers-0r commercial men generally. In the matter of history, Warrington can go along way back. It io midway trrtureen Manchester and Liverpml, hall-way on the London and North Western main-line betwren London and filasthw, and is onved by the above comp my, the Midland Railwny Company, the lire. Wertern Railway CLimpany, the Great Northern It olwiy Company, the Great Central Railway Company and the Cheshire Lines Railway Com- mitur—quite a unique nerviCr 1.4 on moderato exed • town. It used t he a famous plate of call for ih • mail CoSeillse between Marieberter and Lie sm.', • tic• tch limed traveller, at the etid of the but century, writing of Wwrington district, war mint struck with the gibbets whith adorned the roadside. From tbes. gibbets hung the corpses m m who had robbed the mail. on t heir way to and Iron the onrt of Liverpool. streets made the chief characteristic of old Warringt and solar as the centre of the town is concerned, they remain on Vedas ; but in the olden times people did not seem to object to be cramped tor elbow rosin. In fact, the or:biter. of Warrington. Ilk. those of most old lownromem to have conspired to prevent min much light and air from circulating in the otrreta. The buildings on either side seemed afraid to be repot- atrol for fear of being lonely. The traffic caused In the mail cosches and piwt carriaxes passing through War- rington between Londe., and Scotland made the old tr.wn seem extremely bury, and it is • weeder that the stones of Warrington street. did not rise in mutiny ! After all, those Mones bad not ao much to complain 01 as the unlucky pedestrians who hobbled neer them. for they were of the cobble order. Martinecn owes much oth. commercial importance to being built On the river Mersey. Hot how different was the river in the day., of yore trim what .t is to day A clear rippling stream it was when our great grandl ethers used to linger on it. banks on fine ...miter evenings, but there Is now ao temptation to linger there. lithe river has not im • proved in appearance or odour—end I haven't found even the proverbial °ldea inhabitant ir eontend on ouch—tie public buildings of the town have done their best to make up for the river&amp; retrogreesion. What Warrington haa gained cernmercinlly from the meana of trainman provided by the river has been incressed by the construction of the kit...cheater Ship Canal Therefore it might slowed he mud. as Stmkeepeare put It Rich- mond is on the SPAS." rite Warrington of to.l my is re- markable forhothouring &amp; great variety of manufactures Few other tow. in Rine nil of similar sa. can bout of ouch • variety if staple industries." o The history of amps-stove trade Is • brief onedind doer; mat partake of • niedheval character. The old- fashioned cooks would have laughed at the idea of cook- ing by g is, aed even in the early days of gee stoves the ilea wan PI r.j,t to a great deal of ridicule. The first stoves for iffiliaing the heat derived from gas were made about 30 to 60 years ago. The fint really practicable stows route made by • Mr. Shame, of Southampton,but his initial moults were crude indeed compared with the Moves sow turned nut at the Academy-street works. Within the put four years the use of gee stoves and Art, haa largely increased owing in • great ineuure to the use .4 penny.in-the-alot tnetero by the sari.s gaa corn- ponies and Town Council committee Richmond's par. tlespate extensively 10 this new-fashioned method of business ben.. certain claws ii( their cookers are 1110 beat adapted to the requirements of the gm companies' humbler customer.. It Is due to this convenient condi- tion that Mir firm him within the three put years arm- plied one London gee company with over 15.000 c.kers to be hired out. The gm companies have for some sears hero alive to ith advantages as a moven, earner, and they have encouraged its • Sou• TS 1-30</p>
