      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-10-29_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ALLKOKD ENCILOACIIMKNT AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>THE ALLKOKD ENCILOACIIMKNT AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Sir,--Once upon • time there w..at&amp;oeol people dwelling near Warrington who were known as the Heathen.. Now, some nt these Heathens espied a piece of land which memed to them woridroun fair, but an them was a fence round about it, the people could only VA at it and Mere. Now, some of them people re- membered that their forefathers nail this piece of land wan at one time in their possession,until mese rich men came sod presented it to another rich man. on the plea that he that bath much shall have more. Now, the poor who fi d their cattle on the wayside of this land tnur. mured and mid these thing. ought not to be,hut the rich men did not hearken unto thomand having lands or their own and dining auteptuously every day, could not un- derstand why any one should murmur. They a tripled the prude ti be untent with the lot which they de- clared Providence had placed them in, and pointed to themselven as • noble example of whom the people Mould I,llow. Strange ho say the people still murmured and were discontented. how at last there have mimeo mighty men of valour in that part who are noted for candour and holderse of speech, and they have the audacity and Impudence of saying thin land should he returned unto the pa yileNow,the rich man mid,..l have entered into a compact with three in authority over you to give you non peace-offering sufficient land for you to provide yr:under, with a beautiful foothpath, which Mall Le a privilege for ynu to walk upon. !Cm Rome of the people have risen amsinst the men in authority and nat" we will not ratify the Compact an it is an eighteen' oar, nee, and we will bare that back which we think Is our own and we can make footpath. and twilit or sell as seenieth tout just." Now some of the men in authority were exceedingly wroth and -hulas,' all man- ner of things and any the people do not know what Is good fir them, and therefore they .11.11 do what Memeth to them beet. And verily some of the people are submitting tamely and bowing their necks to be trod upon, and believe the wonderful stories that are being told to them an to the beautiful footpath that will be prepared for them. Others will fall down cod vodship the rich men, as they would do unto a graven image. And all these things will he done in the name of justice and liberty.—Yours, hr..</p>
      </div>
