        <head>NARLESTOWN AND NEWFON</head>
        <p>NARLESTOWN AND NEWFON</p>
        <p>•11RAW,. 11 At the Police Station, Earlentown, on Thutnday I morning fief°, Mr. C. B F. Barron, F.ederick ' &amp;nit h, b Manchester, wan chive with bluing in Karlestowo, on 11 winenday morning, and waa fined 2s. GI and cost,.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall. Newton, on Saturday moaning bafere Mr. T. J. Oilleepie, Patrick fluency, farm la- bourer, of Low., was charged with being drunk in Rail way-atreet, garlestown on Friday night, ant wa, tined ho. and Cents. 511,000 DICATII AT EARLIOITOWN. On Sunday morning Mr. Henry Prollipe, watch- nastier, Wargrave.road, died very suddenly at the age of 56 years. He had been unwell t eeveral day. but not auffilient to render medical aid nee.- , nary. On awakening on Sunday morning he seined hie wife to bring up a cup of tea, and on putting it robin lips he tell hack and expired immediately. The facts were reported to the Coroner, who on the representation of Dr. Valentine decided that an in- queet wan unnecessary. Ti,. deceased, who had • place of bum.. in Earle.st., wan very well known, and much sympathy wan expreesed for the family. Ile line henna re•ident of learlestown for come yearn, coming there from Coventry. The cense of death was failure nf the hectic action. The inter- ment took place at the Cemetery on Wednesday,the Sew P. K. Panemore officiating. -</p>
        <p>Goon Tim MART. The usual weekly session of the Hope of Earlea- town Lodge, was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday night. The programme for thin evening wan" Entertainment by the Ju•enilei '' it being their Sikh anniversary the lodge giving them a free tea. The Superintendent (Bro. E. IS ewmani, presided until the diatrict superintendent arrived (Slater A. Duncan) of Liverpool. The programme included the following item. ;---Piano solo, Bro. Roberts ; reading, Slater 11. Frith ; song, Sister Mrs. flail; recitation, Bro. J. Wilson ; recitation, Bro. M. Burke ; solo by Bro. (i. Clarke ; dialogue by 13 rnembera entitled "Sign the Pledge ;" piano solo, Bro. 11. Roberta ; address by Sister Mrs. .1. A. Lee , P.D. VT. of Liverpool ; recitation by Sister Thompson. The 11,11 wit packed.—The usual busi- nenr of the Lodge wnn afterwards gone through. Four candidates were admitted to mernbernhip and several proposed. Greetings were received from Driffield Goifge, Yorkohire. Thanka were tendered to all who took part in the concert on Saturday to celebrate the anniverdary. Staters Duncan and Lee alce gave short aildreaww. The lodge domed in the ugual way, a very enjoyable evening having been spent. -</p>
