        <head>ANOTtiER VICIO-Iti;1711 W.kI2RINOTUN.</head>
        <p>ANOTtiER VICIO-Iti;1711 W.kI2RINOTUN.</p>
        <p>I The large number of i,pirto,s rune 2000 of more—which followed the Warrington team so St. I Helens on Saturday must not only have been. source of encouragement to the Warrington team. but also a mean, of replenishing, to some extent, the dimininhed exchequer of the St. Helens Club. This season the St. Helena Club fa hard pressed by the Bt. Selene Recreation, which has now hoth • Hubby and Association clubs cynnected with itAnd I as a con.quence the Recreation ground ...pied every Sat urday. result.., in II diminiatool gate for the St. Selena town club. Th. counter-attraction • was the means of confusing a number of Warring- . ton supporters who, in mistake, found their way to • the Recreation ground, but the majority of them heat. hardy retreat, minus, however, I ie sdovission : money they had paid. Despite the large influx of Wamngtouians, the 't gate " Was not an exception- ally go of one, considering the position held by it. listens in the Northern Cnian. The day was beau- tifully floe, the turf in goal condition, and every- thing pointed to fine game being witnessed. This proved tube the case, and • better exposition of the Rugby game could belly be wished for. The teanie wery lollows</p>
        <p>—Tia;Cing—tnn,-11.oncow, back . Bahr', Crow, Whor- -1 wood, and Moores, thwc-quarters; War, and f I iggm- bottom half-backs; Taylor, Dakin, Naylor, &amp;holt, Fairhurst, 8/trout, Hill, and Thornton, forwards. It ((chow : Fuulitho, back ; D whorty, Traynor. ! Khrebaw. awl Whahley. thrcequartnra . Crow and I Brows, half backs ; Sudlow, W. Wanstwiley. T Wio ! canloy.Carony,osle,Thompron,Simoooooo.l Brown. I bill, forwards. . . • -</p>
        <p>It will be seen that the Warrington team carnet out as selected. St. Helens, on the other handovere abort of the services of O'Hara Si hall beck, who has not yet recovered Iron a recent injury. Crone —a player of long standing - was drafted from the threequartar line to fill the vacancy, and Whiteley was taken from the forward ranks to take the pities a Cross in the threequarter line. Su.llow.formerly of Warrington, was tee substitute in the forwuds. Bate having won the toes, elected It play wit the wind in his favour lathe first half, and Traynor started operations by kicking off for St. Helens. iligginhottom ...unload the kick, and for an obstruction by Simpson Warring- ton were granted a penalty kick. Several utimmagen followed in the St. Helene 2A, and the visucint showed great determination to croot the line. Their efforts were to be shortly and very un- expectedly rewarded. Bate got 1.108.0ni,, scrimmage, and feigning to pus to Barber, he tricked Briers, end then gave to Barber who dented over the lice without opposition near the corner. This esrly nucceee gave the large flintier of War. ringtonians an opportunity of ...reining their lungs, which it msy be readily imagined, was taken advantage of to eh.- full. Taylor war entrusted with the ensuing kick but failed. Thus within four infantee from the Uric. Warrington had a lead at three points From the drop out Fairhurst made his mark. The enuing play upon f nand Warring. ton hovering round the home lines, and it was only the safe tackling of the tit, Helens men which stayed further scoring. Briers brought relief to • site by intercepting • peu, but the invasion into Warrington urntory wan only momentary, for on Isherwood gaining poseeeelon he ran strongly and passed to Barber, who smartly punted ace°ic the field, the ball jut dropping over the line. Dation dutiful up and se teed, Baiter haying in the meantime pi•Cßil hill I ...wards on sae. The goal kick we, taken lo Barker who &amp;erring/ref the scoot by two point. with a magnificent goat. Till. second reverse thoroughly roused the st. Helene men, and • cornbineti movement by the home t hue- quarter line Ruud them enn.iderallin ground, but Bovow nolllll.o the AP/snug- bye lofty kick. A pretty piece of play by Bate was next witnessed. From a senor/sue, the St. Helenc nun had gained poesessiou,•nd were initiating • passing movement when Bate neatly intercept of, and raced for the line. On reaching Foulkee, fie kickel the ball into the open with, view to Barber gaining poen...ion, but Traynor ju.d succeeded in kicking dee&amp; A O,lmm/um took place near the line, and relief win effected by Cross and Brier'n who worked the ball away. Bate and Iliggintinttoni were inetrunientel In taking the ball back again, •nd Foulkes was called upon to kick nicer hie own goal line in order to saee. From the ...ulna scrimmage, Briers W. again the saviour of his suie, and Of /Hod relief by a smart run and kick The remainder of the play lathe first hell win of • give and take nature, and at half.timo Warrington led by one goal, two trie On points), to it. Helens nil. _</p>
        <p>From the restart, the Mt. Iltdene forwara, played with conaiderable dash. awl carried play to near the Warrin,:ton line. The horn., three-quarter men were now given @ewers' opportunitien to show their reeling abilities. Out they ignomininualy owing ton lack of combination. A free kick in ! front of the posts eran also very (nett' y tales n up by rouge..., who was expected to ...wily kicks goal. The Warrington men wore pieced on tile aggreasive by Taylor who enitisted • paroling movement taken part why Bate, Cron., and Barber, the latter player 'ailing to hold at the critical moment, and the ball went into touch. Vet. /host' x alter wants Barber gained possereion, and panned ft Bate who had the usinfortune to stu tnbte when w.thin a yard of the line. The Bt. Helene forwards again came to the assistance of their silo and forced Warrington beck. A line dribble between Brownbill and Briere pluckily nt14,4 by Be icow close to the War- rington line. I'm. Wrt old igenl to touch down for Warrington...l from the drop out Traynor got pont seseion,and evading Moores he aenred at the corner. The IMMO player ventured at the kick but failed. 9r. Helens now made vigorou. efforts to fetch Op he leeway and prtseed Warrington severely. Moore, was conspicuous by line defensive play, awl on several °octagon. he brilliantly relieved by (farth- ing along the tou.th line. A grand passing move- ment bet wnen Crorm.lshet wootlAnd Moore*, carried play to near the St Helen. line. The venue was, however, changed by the St Helena forward, who played a grand game,and when the whistle sounded for time Ht. Helens were wain promoting. Tbe anal acorn Wan Warrington nne goal two true. 0 plitltS). It. II elon• Or". try 13 pnin,s).</p>
